  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 206 PM CDT Fri Oct 30 2026\n\nEarly afternoon analysis showed a surface warm front just south of \nthe NE/KS border that will continue to edge northward tonight and \nthrough the day Saturday. North of the front, there is currently a \nline of showers and storms associated with some isentropic ascent \nand low level warm air advection. These will continue to push north \nthis afternoon, exiting to the north by around 8 PM. Meanwhile, some \nadditional precip development is expected as the surface front \npushes through along with some shortwave energy currently over \nsouthwest KS. This precip doesn't look to reach our south until \naround 9 PM, but will overspread much of the area overnight into \nearly Saturday. Latest short term guidance suggests we'll see on and \noff precip through the day on Saturday, with model soundings \noscillating in and out of saturation, with potential for both \nshowers and drizzle at times. In addition, can't completely rule out \na few rumbles of thunder Saturday morning like we had this morning, \nthough instability will remain pretty limited once again. The light \nprecip could linger into Halloween evening, but not expecting it to \nbe too impactful. Sunday will be similar with on and off light \nprecip, but weak upper level ridging sliding through should yield \nmore dry time than wet. Otherwise, expect highs in the 60s both days.\n\nAttention then turns to higher and more widespread rain chances to \nstart next week. A cutoff low currently of the Pacific Northwest \ncoast will move into the Desert Southwest Sunday and phase up with a \ntrough currently well out over the Pacific. A surface low will then \nspin up east of the Rockies and push through the Plains on Monday \nand eventually toward MN by Tuesday/Wednesday. Still some model \nspread on exact track and speed of the low, though consensus takes \nit through or maybe just west of our forecast area. The system looks \nto bring widespread rainfall to the area, potentially heavy at \ntimes, with NAEFS suggesting precipitable water values of 1.5-1.75\", \ngood for the 99th+ percentile or even max of climatology. Still a \nlot of details to refine, but should the system linger in the area \nlonger, some localized flooding could occur. In addition, depending \non track and timing, we could see a severe thunderstorm threat with \nvarious ensemble members suggesting potential for a plume of decent \ninstability ahead of the system along with the almost certain strong \nvertical shear.\n\nAssociated precip should exit by late Tuesday or Wednesday with a \ncold front passing through and strong northwest winds on the back \nside of the system ushering in some slightly cooler air. Still, \ndaily highs look to remain in the 50s, near or even above average \nfor this time of year, with lows in the mid 30s to lower 40s. A few \nvarious ensemble members also suggest some weaker shortwave energy \nsliding through later in the week, giving us continued 15-20%\nchances for precip, but a lot of it will be driven by how the \nmain early- week system behaves.",
